      faqs: [{"q":"What is the standard Open Graph image size in 2026?","a":"The widely published Open Graph standard is 1200 by 630 pixels, a ratio of about 1.91 to 1. Use this size for link preview images on Facebook, LinkedIn, X, WhatsApp, and other platforms, so your links show a large, sharp preview card when shared."},{"q":"What size should an Instagram post be in 2026?","a":"Use 1080 by 1080 pixels for a square post, 1080 by 1350 pixels for a portrait post, and 1080 by 566 pixels for a landscape post. For your profile picture, upload a square image of at least 512 by 512 pixels, keep your face centred, and remember it is displayed in a circle."},{"q":"What size is a YouTube thumbnail in 2026?","a":"A YouTube thumbnail is 1280 by 720 pixels, a 16:9 ratio. Keep text large and readable at small sizes, keep faces and key elements in the centre, and export as JPG for a photograph or PNG for a graphic with sharp text."},{"q":"Can I use the same image size for every social platform?","a":"No, because each platform uses different shapes and sizes. A square image will be cropped on YouTube, and a wide image will be cropped as a profile picture. Resize the same original separately for each placement, and keep text and faces in the centre so cropping does not remove them."},{"q":"How do I resize an image for social media without distortion?","a":"Lock the aspect ratio when the target shape matches the original, and crop when the shape must change, instead of stretching the image. Always resize from the original in one step, preview the cropped result, and export as JPG for photos or PNG for graphics with transparency."},{"q":"Do social media image sizes change over time?","a":"Yes, platforms update their layouts, so a size that was correct last year may be cropped differently this year. The sizes in this guide are widely published standards, but if an image suddenly looks wrong, check the platform current recommended size before assuming your file is broken."}],
